{{wikipedia}}
===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno}} {{term|parlance|lang=xno}}, {{term|parlaunce}}, from {{term|parler||to talk}} + {{term|-ance}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈpɑː.ləns/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɑɹ.ləns/}}
* {{audio|en-us-parlance.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A certain way of [[speak]]ing, of using words, especially when it comes to those with a particular [[job]] or interest.
#*'''1836''', [[w:James Fenimore Cooper|James Fenimore Cooper]], "[[s:Eclipse|Eclipse]]",
#*:To my childish fancy, it had seemed an imaginary flag-staff, or, in rustic '''parlance''', the "liberty pole" of some former generation {{...}}
# {{archaic|rare}} Speech, [[discussion]] or [[debate]].
#*{{quote-book|1849|[[w:Lady Charlotte Guest|Lady Charlotte Guest]]|''[[w:The Mabinogion|The Mabinogion]]''|section="[[s:The_Mabinogion/Peredur_the_Son_of_Evrawc|Peredur the Son of Evrawc]]"|passage=And without further parlance they fought, {{...}}}}
#*{{quote-book|1909|[[w:William Elliot Griffis|William Elliot Griffis]]|The Story of New Netherland|section=[[s:The_Story_of_New_Netherland/Chapter_22|Chapter 22]]|passage=The tourist's impression of the country to-day is that of a transported Holland, in which the official language is Dutch and the parlance of the people is "taki-taki."}}

